== 2. RULING 1 — red 3 is a genuine red; the detector, not the red, is wrong ==

The log body's only FAIL row is `unreferenced brief is reached by checkStudio []` — an
assertion failure with a printed actual value, no stack, no loader error. The exit 1 is that
row's. The string ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND appears exactly once in the log, on a PASS row, and it
is W-022 behaviour 3's own fixture text asserting that isModuleLoadFailure detects that
string. Red 3 is assertion-level. The advisory is a FALSE POSITIVE and does not count against
this opus.

The detector is a defect, and it is the W-084 class exactly — a scanner beaten by its own
fixture. `rules/evidence.ts:216` runs `isModuleLoadFailure(logText)` over the WHOLE log,
header and PASS rows included, so any log that so much as quotes the string is reclassified as
not-assertion-level. `# command:` lines are in scope too. Class enumeration, searched: all
callers of isModuleLoadFailure (one, line 216) and every regex/`includes` over a whole log
text in check.ts, rules/*.ts and commands/*.ts. ONE instance of the defect. One sibling worth
naming but not the same bug: check.ts:503 accepts a gate log that merely `includes(hash)`
anywhere — loose by design there, since the header's position is not fixed.

Out of scope for W-084 (a different rule, P-005/W-022, with its own behaviours and no red
here); filed as its own item. The remedy is to test only rows that are not `^PASS`, or only
the log body's failing rows.

W-084's own half of the collision, which IS in scope and is 2 lines: W-084's behaviours reuse
the same numeric selector (1-3) as the file's legacy W-022 behaviours (1-6), so `checkW084`
suppresses nothing of the legacy `check()` output and every W-084 red log carries one
unrelated legacy PASS row. Behaviour 3's happens to be the ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND one. A
distinct selector range (W-084 rows selected as 7-9, still recorded `--behaviour 1..3`) keeps
each red log to its own assertion and removes the advisory — without a fourth file, which the
brief does not own.

