== 6. why M3 survives, and the finding underneath it ==

The fenced exclusion is not pinned because the inline exclusion masks it. `citationProse`'s
`.replace(/(`+)[\s\S]*?\1/g, "")` matches across newlines, so with the fence toggle removed the
two ``` marker lines pair up as one "inline code span" and the fenced Behaviour 9 is deleted
anyway. The comment above the function claims inline spans are matched by "any matching
backtick-run length; an unmatched delimiter is prose" — measured on the real corpus, that
across-newline pairing is not narrow:

  prose deleted before the rule ever looks, studio/briefs at HEAD, after fence stripping:
    corpus-wide  22.5%
    W-047.md     81.5%  (35,604 of 43,923 chars) — three single "spans" of 11,320, 6,502 and
                        14,965 chars, each a `` delimiter paired with another `` thousands of
                        characters later:
                        head "`` | `runMerge`/`runBranch` positional | a git **pathspec** …"
                        tail "…adding a\n  raw ``"
    W-050.md     41.1%
    W-033.md     23.1%   W-042.md 27.5%   W-087.md 14.2%

A block rule that is blind over 81.5% of one brief and 22.5% of the corpus has a reach far
narrower than "the three exclusions" describes, and the brief's named limitation (lowercase
forms) is not the big one. Today it costs nothing: bounding spans to a single line, which is
what a Markdown code span is, raises live matches 74 -> 75 and leaves OUT OF RANGE AT 0, so no
residue is being hidden right now. It is a prospective hole and an undocumented one.

Verified remedy, because a wrong recommendation costs a round: replacing the multiline replace
with a per-line `l.replace(/(`+)[^`\n]*?\1/g, "")` leaves evidence.test.ts and rules.test.ts
green (all seven constructs still silent, real corpus still 0), AND makes M3 KILLED with the
existing fixture — one line closes the blind spot and pins the fence branch that For Sol
target 2 asked about.

== 7. findings ==

F1 (fix, blocking this round) — the inline exclusion matches across newlines. §6: 22.5% of the
corpus, 81.5% of W-047, deleted before the rule looks; it also masks the fenced branch, which
is why M3 survives. Verified one-line remedy in §6. If the builder prefers a different fix, the
acceptance is: M3 KILLED, all seven constructs silent, real corpus 0, and the deleted-prose
percentage measured and recorded.

F2 (fix, blocking this round) — behaviour 2's unreadable-brief row does not discriminate (M5).
`unreadable.md` sorts last in safeList's sorted listing and the only brief before it yields no
findings, so aborting the crawl at that entry is indistinguishable from skipping it. The brief
requires "the rest of the crawl completes". Name the entry so it sorts FIRST and assert a
later brief's finding still appears.

F3 (fix, blocking this round) — acceptance criterion 4 is unmet: no census recorded as
evidence, no kept script. §4 has the numbers; the obligation is the opus's, not the censor's.
Keep the script, record raw/live/per-exclusion/out-of-range as a log.

F4 (nit, not blocking) — `safeList` was rewritten off `listMd`. The new body is
`readdirSync().filter(.md).sort().map(join)`, i.e. a copy of the adapter's `listMd`
(adapters/native/src/index.ts:125) minus an existsSync guard the try/catch already covers, and
the `listMd` import was dropped. Functionally inert, but it is a shared helper that
opus.untracked and opus.red_evidence also call, and it now diverges from the three sibling
safeList copies (check.ts:127, rules/process.ts:35, rules/paths.ts:36) that all delegate to
listMd. The brief's house constraints ask for reuse of what the file already does. Restore
`return listMd(dir);`. Searched: every safeList definition and every listMd caller — 1 instance.

F5 (nit, not blocking, recommended) — the red-log selector overlay of §2, which is the
in-scope half of the false positive and is 2 lines.

Not W-084's, filed separately: isModuleLoadFailure applied to whole log text
(rules/evidence.ts:216).

== 8. verdict ==

FAIL, round 1. The build is genuinely good: TDD shape real and re-derived, provenance exact to
the hash, the all-brief crawl proven by a mutation rather than asserted, seven of nine
mutations killed, the corpus green, zero new blocking findings, suites clean. It fails on one
measured precision hole in a BLOCK-level rule (F1), one behaviour whose robustness row cannot
fail (F2), and one unmet acceptance criterion (F3). All three are small and mechanical; one
round should close them.
